{
 "MonitoringProgramDescription": "The Tahoe Region has 151 miles of bicycle and pedestrian facilities: 64 miles of shared-use paths separated from traffic, 47 miles of bike lanes, 10 miles of signed bike routes, and 29 miles of sidewalk.\n\nThis page maps every segment and what is known about it: who maintains it, whether snow is cleared in winter, and when it was built.\n\nAnother 119 miles are proposed in the Active Transportation Plan, 55 of them shared-use paths. Projects are still being built: the layer records paths completed in 2025 and 2026 in the City of South Lake Tahoe and El Dorado County.",
 "MonitoringApproach": "TRPA keeps the network as a map layer of segments, each with a type, a length in miles, the agency that maintains it, and the year it was built. Class 1 is a shared-use path separated from the road, Class 2 a bike lane, and Class 3 a bike route on a shared road, often marked with sharrows. The layer is updated as projects are built, and every four years with the Active Transportation Plan.\n\nThe proposed network is a second layer from the same plan, last updated in spring 2024. It adds two types: sidewalks, and complete streets, where the exact facility is not yet decided. Each segment is in the financially constrained plan, which has funding expected, or the unconstrained plan, which does not.\n\nRead the attributes with care. The layer is kept by hand: agency names are spelled several ways, which the charts read as one, and the year built is free text. Only 87 of the 151 miles have a year; most of the rest say only that they were built before 2006. Winter snow removal is marked not applicable for most bike lanes and routes, which are on roads.",
